<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- LAN connection to the paired computer. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Required to hold a WifiManager.MulticastLock while browsing for the
      desktop over mDNS. Without the lock, Wi-Fi hardware filters multicast
      while the screen is off and discovery silently stops working.

      This permission is also one of the permissions that qualifies a service
      for the `connectedDevice` foreground service type on Android 14+, which
      is the type this app uses. That is not a coincidence or a loophole: we
      hold it because we genuinely use multicast.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE" />

    <!-- The ongoing-connection notification. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- Scanning the pairing QR code. Requested at the moment of use. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      Deliberately absent, and the rule that keeps them so:

        AnyFlow acquires a privileged Android capability only when a named,
        user-visible feature requires it; only through the platform-sanctioned
        API for that feature; only with the user's explicit, separately
        revocable consent; and never as a means of defeating a platform
        restriction that exists to protect the user.

      By that rule these stay out, and no feature is a reason to revisit them,
      because each is either a workaround for a protection or a grant far wider
      than any AnyFlow feature needs:

        * no BIND_ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE
        * no QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES
        * no SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW
        * no READ_LOGS
        * no location permission (this app never needs the user's location)
        * no MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE
        * no default-IME status, no hidden APIs, no reflection, no root
        * no CompanionDeviceManager association (ADR-0015 §10: a live CDM
          association makes this app a *trusted* listener, which switches OFF
          Android's sensitive-content redaction. Adopting it is a notification-
          privacy decision and needs its own ADR, whatever the reason you
          reached for it.)

      BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE used to be on this list, and it moved.
      It is the only entry that has ever moved, and moving it took an ADR
      (ADR-0015). It is declared below on the notification listener service —
      held by the *system*, not by us, exactly as BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE is
      for the clipboard tile — and it is what stops any other app from binding
      that service. Nothing is read until the user grants notification access
      in Settings AND separately grants notifications.v1 to one paired
      computer; either can be withdrawn without the other.

      See docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md and docs/adr/ADR-0015-notification-access.md.
    -->

    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:name=".AnyFlowApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.AnyFlow">

        <activity
            android:name=".ui.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The Sharesheet entry point: Gallery / Files / Browser -> Share ->
          AnyFlow. Exported because that is what makes it appear in the
          chooser; it accepts only a shared stream and does nothing with an
          empty or unrecognised intent.

          `mimeType="*/*"` is what lets any file type be shared. It grants the
          app nothing: the URI arrives with a temporary read permission for
          that one item, which is strictly less access than any storage
          permission would be, and is exactly why no storage permission is
          declared anywhere in this manifest.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.SendActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/share_target_label"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.AnyFlow.Dialog">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              Selected text, shared straight to a paired computer as a
              clipboard update. `text/plain` is listed explicitly even though
              `*/*` already matches it, because Android ranks a share target
              higher when the type is named — and because being explicit is
              what documents that shared text takes the clipboard.v1 path,
              not the files.v1 one.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          `connectedDevice`: this service exists only while an active
          connection to a paired computer exists or is being established. It
          is NOT a `dataSync` daemon and it is not started at boot.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".service.ConnectionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="connectedDevice" />

        <!--
          "Send clipboard" in the Quick Settings panel.

          The tile cannot read the clipboard itself: a TileService has no
          input focus, and since Android 10 that is what `getPrimaryClip`
          requires. It therefore opens MainActivity with an explicit request,
          and the Activity — which does have focus — reads the clipboard after
          the person confirms. That is the supported flow, not a workaround
          for one. See ClipboardTileService.

          BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE is held by the *system*, not by us: it is
          what stops any other app from binding this service.
        -->
        <!--
          notifications.v1 — mirroring this phone's notifications to a paired
          computer. Optional, off by default, and inert on a fresh install.

          `android:permission` names the permission the *system* must hold to
          bind this service. We do not hold it; declaring it is what stops any
          other application from binding us.

          `default_autobind=false` is the load-bearing one. Without it the
          system binds this listener whenever the app is installed and access
          is granted, and AnyFlow would be reading notifications with nobody
          listening. With it, the binding is requested only while a paired
          computer that holds the notifications.v1 grant is connected, and
          released when the last one goes away — which is what makes "AnyFlow
          reads your notifications only while a granted computer is connected"
          structurally true rather than a promise. See ADR-0015 §3.

          `default_filter_types` is a real, free, OS-enforced narrowing that
          sits underneath AnyFlow's own per-app filter: the system delivers
          only conversations and alerting notifications unless the user says
          otherwise. `disabled_filter_types` is deliberately NOT declared —
          it would grey those types out permanently in the system UI, and a
          person who genuinely wants their media notification mirrored should
          be able to have it.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".notifications.AnyFlowNotificationListener"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/notification_listener_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.notification.default_autobind"
                android:value="false" />
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.service.notification.default_filter_types"
                android:value="conversations|alerting" />
        </service>

        <service
            android:name=".ui.ClipboardTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/clipboard_tile_label"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_clipboard"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>
</manifest>
